To solve for \(x\) given the system of linear equations:

\[
\begin{align*}
3x + 4y - 12z &= 10, \tag{1} \\
-2x - 3y + 9z &= -4, \tag{2}
\end{align*}
\]

we will use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate one of the variables, say \(y\), by making the coefficients of \(y\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.

Step 1: Multiply equation (1) by 3 and equation (2) by 4 to make the coefficients of \(y\) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign:

\[
\begin{align*}
3(3x + 4y - 12z) &= 3(10), \\
4(-2x - 3y + 9z) &= 4(-4).
\end{align*}
\]

This simplifies to:

\[
\begin{align*}
9x + 12y - 36z &= 30, \tag{3} \\
-8x - 12y + 36z &= -16. \tag{4}
\end{align*}
\]

Step 2: Add equations (3) and (4) to eliminate \(y\):

\[
\begin{align*}
(9x + 12y - 36z) + (-8x - 12y + 36z) &= 30 + (-16).
\end{align*}
\]

This simplifies to:

\[
\begin{align*}
9x - 8x &= 14, \\
x &= 14.
\end{align*}
\]

Thus, the value of \(x\) is \(\boxed{14}\).

